WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.025 --> 00:00:04.250
[Music starts]

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:09.819
[Audio Description - not captioned in this video]

00:00:09.819 --> 00:00:14.719
[Drayana Dunson] I've been through&nbsp;
18 foster homes, five group homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:19.443
and five shelters - five different&nbsp;
shelters - in the past 13 years.

00:00:20.285 --> 00:00:25.860
[Amyia Berry] My parents had recently&nbsp;
had got a divorce. I would have like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:31.093
bad thoughts and would end up actually&nbsp;
here in Cincinnati Children's a lot.

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:39.833
So, that's when they decided that it would be&nbsp;
better for me to go to the foster care system.

00:00:45.061 --> 00:00:49.930
[Judith W Dexheimer, PhD] So, there are&nbsp;
about 470,000 children in foster care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:57.000
across the United States. And of kids in&nbsp;
foster care, they have disjointed medical care.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.286
And when they change placements,&nbsp;
or change to a new foster family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.286 --> 00:01:05.340
they frequently change and potentially&nbsp;
lose their medical records.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.627
[Dunson] When your caseworkers drop you off, they&nbsp;
don't give them the information that they need.&nbsp;

00:01:09.627 --> 00:01:12.633
They just let them know "hey,&nbsp;
got this child, she's a female,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:16.900
she's this age, and we'll be bringing&nbsp;
her in. Do you have any beds open?"

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.835
[Dexheimer] So, I applied for an NLM grant ...

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:29.460
... because what the NLM is trying&nbsp;
to do, with health disparities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.320
and health disparities research, fits directly&nbsp;
into what we want to do with our care.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.580
Our goal was to start with&nbsp;
the kids who were 18 and over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.353
who are eligible for, or emancipating out of care,

00:01:45.127 --> 00:01:51.960
and help provide them with both their medical&nbsp;
records, and a portion of their data from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.760
job and family services - so from the&nbsp;
people who have their actual custody.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.833
So, they could see their records. They could see&nbsp;
their major diagnoses, their current medications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:07.860
their immunizations. And then then they&nbsp;
could become stewards of their own data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.100
but then they also had it in&nbsp;
a form that they could take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.380
with them as they transition&nbsp;
from pediatric to adult care.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.126
[Dunson] One of the reasons I use Health Hero&nbsp;
was to set up my appointment for my pregnancy.

00:02:20.126 --> 00:02:23.500
I did not remember all&nbsp;
my testing that I had done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.820
cause I never had like actual&nbsp;
copy of my insurance card.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:31.280
So, my insurance card was on there and they&nbsp;
can scan it and put it through their system.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.167
If you don't know your information by heart, it&nbsp;
will be there for you to remember and it just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:41.693
- it helps. It shows you more information that&nbsp;
you can learn outside of what people tells you.

00:02:41.751 --> 00:02:45.381
[Dexheimer] One of the great things&nbsp;
about doing the funding through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.381 --> 00:02:49.167
National Library of Medicine&nbsp;
is the primary focus was how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:55.553
to build an application with the&nbsp;
end users involved in a novel way.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.459
How to get it out to them so that they&nbsp;
could put it in their hands again and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:09.240
then how to evaluate whether or not they used it.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:15.420
[Berry] A feature on Health Hero, that was&nbsp;
helpful to me, was under immunization records.&nbsp;

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:22.980
I was able to login, look at my records, and&nbsp;
print it out to get into nursing school.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.867
I grew up in the hospital and&nbsp;
that made me want to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:33.294
a nurse because I would become very&nbsp;
close to the nurse. I would just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.294 --> 00:03:37.520
to take care of people for the rest&nbsp;
of my life if I ever get the chance.

00:03:37.793 --> 00:03:41.727
[Dunson] It's important for youth that's&nbsp;
being emancipated to have Health Hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.727 --> 00:03:45.973
because of the fact that it's just&nbsp;
something that you need to know.

00:03:45.973 --> 00:03:50.053
It's - you're not going to always have someone&nbsp;
there that remembers all your information.

00:03:50.668 --> 00:03:54.399
My advice, to a person that was&nbsp;
going through what I went through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.399 --> 00:04:03.120
is you never give up. You don't let the foster&nbsp;
care make you who you are. You always fight and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.600
you don't give up. You don't let the foster&nbsp;
care define you. You don't let it break you.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.870
[Music and audio description]

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.494
[Music fades to silence]

